You draw on the gate-guard's stamina — the old breath they teach you the first day, the one that keeps you standing at your post when the fight's gone long — and your ribs knit some of their ache (Second Wind, a bonus action: 7 healed). A little strength back. Then you swing at the thing.

It doesn't dodge. Your blade goes through it clean, its light flickering around the steel like water around a dropped stone, and the longsword bites nothing at all. Iron does not touch it. You've swung at smoke. It hangs there, unimpressed, a hair's breadth from killing you, so bright now the whole gallery is noon around you.

Then it strikes again — a second bolt of white lightning (its Shock, 6 lightning damage) that locks your teeth and sets your armor ringing, though you keep your feet. You're at six of your fourteen strength, up but thin, and your sword arm has just been proven useless to you. The Anvil Father teaches patience, not pride: the walker is not a thing a hammer kills.

Behind you, Doran is screaming it in his ruined whisper: "The stair! Bright light, Brakka — torches! It flinches from a big flame — get us to the stair, get us climbing!"

You've a backpack with ten torches, cold and dark. Your shuttered lantern, a stamp of your boot from open. The stair back up to the daylight is behind you past the landing — maybe eighty feet of running, downhill footing first, with a floating death at your shield-rim. Doran's leg is freed but stiff; he'll move slow, limping on your shoulder.

Your turn, Brakka. Your action, your movement. What do you do?


