The torch catches — a guttering, resinous roar of real flame — and you sweep it at the walker as you turn to run.

You never make a step. As you pivot away it strikes you in the back of the shoulder — an opportunity attack as you leave its reach, rolling high, its Shock for 9 lightning damage — and every muscle you own lets go at once. That takes you from six strength to none: down to 0, senseless on the mine floor. The torch rolls from your fist, still burning, guttering against the stone.

You are down, Brakka. Your eyes are open but the world is grey water; you feel grit under your cheek, feel your heart still hammering, feel nothing else obey you. Somewhere above you the pale light hangs close, swelling bright, then dim, then bright — patient, like a smith deciding when the iron's done.

But the Anvil Father's forge doesn't go cold this easily. Death reaches for you and finds dwarf-iron: your heart holds its beat. One step back from the dark — a death saving throw, and the die came up an 18. One success of the three you need.

And there is Doran. Three days down a hole have made him thin as wire, but terror gives a man strength, and he lurches out from behind the rubble, snatches up your fallen torch, and plants himself over your body like a shepherd with a crook of fire, sweeping the flame in great arcs at the light. "You don't get him! NOT HIM!" His voice is torn metal and prayer both, and the walker flinches wide around the fire's bright reach, its glow guttering and swelling in fury, circling, looking for the gap where a torch can't sweep.

You are unconscious; the dice will ask again on your next turn. Doran holds the torch above you. What does Brakka do — anything he'd mutter, or pray, or is there anything within reach of a twitching hand?


>>>>>> round 10, the player: I can't move, but with whatever's left in me I try to keep my grip on the holy symbol at my chest and silently pray to the Anvil Father: iron that holds, hold me. I leave the rest to Doran and the dice.

  [notebook: dismissed 1 name]
