Your fingers won't close, not truly, but they don't have to: the little struck-anvil has hung at your chest through six years of gate watches, and it is lying over your heart right now, cold iron on the drum of it. Iron that holds, Father. Hold me. And the hammer that keeps your blood moving answers in its own slow way — another beat, then another.

Above you the fight goes strangely. The walker circles Doran's sweeping torch at the very rim of its light, swelling bright, guttering dark, swelling again — furious, but it will not come into the flame's bright reach while Doran swings it, and Doran swings it like a man threshing grain, roaring through cracked lips. Each time the light feints toward your body, the fire sweeps across and it flows back into the dark, patient as rust.

"Get up," Doran grinds through his teeth, hauling you by the collar a few feet at a time along the gallery floor toward the landing, torch never still. "Get UP, gate-guard. The Father didn't send you down here to lie down here."

And your heart hears something in that. Your next death saving throw is rolled this turn: the d20 comes up 19 against DC 10 — a success, recorded on your sheet, making two of the three needed. You are not out of the dark yet, but you are one success from waking, and the dice will ask again on your next turn.

The stair mouth is still seventy feet off. Doran can't haul you faster and hold the torch both; the walker circles, waiting for a dropped arm. What does Brakka do? — anything at all, while the dark asks its question again: a word, a breath, a prayer, a deed within reach of a twitching hand.


>>>>>> round 11, the player: I can't do more than breathe, but I try to rasp out a word to Doran: "Torches... in my pack. Light another, keep it in your off hand." I keep my fist closed on the anvil symbol and hold on to the pulse in my chest.

  [dice Brakka's death saving throw (DC 10), round 4: 1d20 = 1d20 [18] = 18]
  [sheet: death save successes: 3/3; three death save successes: stable (unconscious at 0 HP, no death saves; regains 1 HP after 1d4 hours unless healed); death save counts reset]

Your rasp barely carries — "Torches... in my pack..." — and it's a wonder Doran hears it at all over his own roaring. But a miner's ear is tuned to a voice through rock, and this one has spent three days listening to the dark. His hand shoots out, hooks the flap of your pack, drags a fresh torch from the bundle, and touches it to the burning one. resin catches; now he has a brand in each fist, and he stands them wide apart, sweeping in slow great arcs, so the light around you is one moving flame with no gap in it.

The walker flares with fury at the edge of it — swelling blinding, guttering, swelling again — but two fires leave no gap for its patience to seep through. All it can do is circle.

And Brakka, the dark asks its question one more time, and your heart gives the same answer it always has — another beat. That's the third success, and the dice are done asking: you are stable, your life no longer bleeding away. But stable is not awake. Bleeding is stopped; you lie at the end of your strength, and without healing, you'll lie there senseless for hours, not waking before those hours pass. (Stable at 0 HP, unconscious; the fight no longer rolls death saves for you.)

"Steady now, Brakka. Steady," Doran says, right above you, opening up his sweeping rhythm to the wider sweep needed to carry him and the weight of his friend toward the stair at once. "I can't run and carry you both. I need to get you to the stair and I need to shout for help that can't come until morning, when the masons show to brick us in." He barks a raw laugh at the joke at his own expense. Brakka — you're breathing, and the dice can't do more for you, only Doran can. What happens next is his turn and yours; what does Brakka do?


